[0.001, "o", "\u001b[33m╭─\u001b[0m\u001b[33m───\u001b[0m\u001b[33m MEDIUM: High Cross-Origin Risk: Many MCP Servers Connected [AG-024] \u001b[0m\u001b[33m────\u001b[0m\u001b[33m─╮\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m Agent: \u001b[1menterprise-agents\u001b[0m                                                     \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m                                                                              \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m Agent 'enterprise-agents' connects to 5 MCP servers. The number of potential \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m cross-origin paths grows exponentially with each additional server.          \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m                                                                              \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m With 5 servers, there are 10 unique server pairs — each a potential          \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m cross-origin escalation path.                                                \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m                                                                              \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m Palo Alto Unit 42 fo"]
[0.000, "o", "und 78.3% attack success rate with 5 connected servers.  \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m                                                                              \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m \u001b[2mAttack:\u001b[0m Each server pair creates a trust boundary crossing opportunity. An   \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m attacker only needs to exploit ONE server to pivot across all others via the \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m agent acting as a confused deputy.                                           \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m \u001b[2mBlast radius:\u001b[0m All 5 servers' resources are interconnected through the agent. \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m \u001b[2mOWASP Agentic:\u001b[0m ASI07 Insecure Inter-Agent Communication · ASI03 Identity &   \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m Privilege Abuse                                                              \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m                                                                              \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m \u001b[32mFix:\u001b[0m 1. Minimize the number of MCP servers per agent (principle of least  "]
[0.000, "o", "   \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m privilege)                                                                   \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m 2. Use separate agents for separate concerns (don't connect filesystem +     \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m database + network to ONE agent)                                             \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m 3. Implement cross-server access policies (Server A's data can't flow to     \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m Server B)                                                                    \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m 4. Add data-flow boundaries between server contexts                          \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m│\u001b[0m \u001b[2mLocation:\u001b[0m examples/enterprise-agents/mcp.json                                \u001b[33m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\u001b[0m\r\n"]
